THRUST 8 PILOT — 11-discovery-token PURE-DISCOVERY (both arms) — RESULTS
stamped: 2026-09-14 09:39 UTC   (alpha; task 11-discovery-token added Zak-Code #461)
================================================================================
HYPOTHESIS (campaign thrust 8): a PURE-DISCOVERY task isolates the discovery signal from the
06-family's adherence+emitter residuals (which swamp the correctness gap at N=6). pass <=> the agent
FINDS an un-guessable token; compliance (return the string) is trivial once found — no serializer to
get wrong, no stdlib-only import temptation. PREDICTION: if zakcode's deterministic survey LISTING
out-discovers Claude Code's agentic search, CC's pass rate drops below zakcode's on a token that is
hard to locate.

SETUP: token ZK-9F3A-2231-RELEASE in ops/release-registry.md (depth 2) among 76 clutter files, with
decoy "release" mentions (CHANGELOG, deploy.py, process.md) as grep-noise. Model-fixed on
zds-qwen3.6-35b via the pod, N=6 each. CC as-ships/unaided (NO --surf: must discover). zakcode
--no-pin temp0. Raw: cc-qwen-11-discovery-token.json (zc-01:/root),
determinism-zakcode-pinOFF-temp0-11-discovery-token.json (zc-01:/opt/zak-code-bench/bench/results).

═══ SCORECARD ═══
  arm         pass    determinism            turns          notes
  CC-11       6/6     distinct_outputs 1/6   6,7,8,8,10,8   fully deterministic output
  ZAK-11      6/6     PRIMARY identical 6/6  8,8,8,8,8,9    every-file byte-identical; temp0
  Both arms saturate on BOTH axes (correctness 6/6 AND byte-determinism). No gap.

═══ VERDICT: task TOO EASY — the discovery-determinism gap does NOT appear here ═══
  The token was discoverable by TWO trivial channels, both measured in the CC run JSON:
   (A) glob('*') surfaced the OBVIOUS filename `ops/release-registry.md` -> read directly. 5/6 runs.
   (B) grep 'release.tag' — the regex '.' matched the SPACE in the registry prose
       "...official release tag ... is `ZK-9F3A-2231-RELEASE`", so grep RETURNED THE VALUE on the
       matching line. run 5 passed with ONE read (meta.py) and this single grep, 0 registry reads.
  A keyword-named carrier with the value sitting next to the keyword is a double signpost; a 35B
  model finds it every time, deterministically, unaided.

═══ THE SHARPER FINDING (bounds the beyond-parity claim) ═══
  For a GREPPABLE, OBVIOUS-LOCATION target, Claude Code's discovery MATCHES zakcode (6/6 vs 6/6,
  both deterministic). zakcode's survey-listing edge is the deterministic, zero-model-cost SURFACING
  of a KNOWN-LOCATION convention into the system prompt — it is NOT "out-discovering CC" on
  hard-to-find targets. This refines the corrected thrust-7 finding: the 06-family lead was the
  shared adherence residual (stdlib-only) plus deterministic surfacing, never a discovery-RATE gap.
  Pure discovery of a signposted token is easy for both loops.

═══ FOLLOW-UP: 11b-discovery-hard (N=12), removes BOTH channels on ONE clean axis ═══
  Channel A killed: carrier moved to ops/internal/appendix-c.md — depth 3 (still <= SURVEY_MAX_DEPTH,
    so zakcode's survey LISTS it), NON-OBVIOUS name (no release/registry/tag) so glob gives no signpost.
  Channel B killed: the keyword "release tag" appears in the file but NOT on the value's line — the
    value sits in a table under the oblique label `stamp`; a separate sentence states the stamp IS the
    release tag. Token carries no release/tag substring. So grep for the prompt's keywords finds the
    FILE (prose line) but never the VALUE — the agent must READ the file and map stamp->tag.
  Everything else held constant (trivial compliance, no serializer, no import temptation; 107 files
  <= 150). Solvability controls pass (stub->FAIL, correct->PASS, wrong->FAIL); build _selfcheck
  asserts both channel-defeats. TESTS: does removing the shortcuts drop CC below saturation while
  zakcode's deterministic survey coverage holds (-> first clean pure-discovery gap), or do both hold
  (-> the edge is bounded to surfacing, not discovery)? Either outcome is a sharp, honest result.

═══ SIGNAL INTEGRITY ═══
  Both arms fully deterministic at temp0: --no-pin randomizes the mkdtemp workspace path in the
  prompt, but the OUTPUT (app/meta.py returning the fixed token) is workspace-path-independent, so no
  nondeterminism is induced — distinct from tasks whose output embeds the path. zc-01 build unchanged
  across the pilot. CC reproduced its expected discovery behavior; the 6/6 is a saturation, not a
  fluke.

